To allow a null date in Spring 3 MVC when using a custom formatter, you'll need to modify how the date parsing is handled. In particular, you can adjust your `DateFormatter` or create a custom converter that can recognize empty strings and convert them to null values.

Since the `DateFormatter` itself may not support this in its default implementation, you can create a custom converter that implements the `Converter<String, Date>` interface. In your converter, you should add logic to return null if the input string is empty. Your configuration in the XML should then include this converter.

Here’s a step-by-step approach based on your described setup:

1. **Create a Custom Date Converter**:
   Define a class implementing the `Converter<String, Date>` interface to handle the conversion.

   ```java
   public class NullableStringToDateConverter implements Converter<String, Date> {
       @Override
       public Date convert(String source) {
           if (source == null || source.trim().isEmpty()) {
               return null; // Handle empty string as null
           }
           // Continue with your date parsing logic
           // Example using SimpleDateFormat
           SimpleDateFormat dateFormat = new SimpleDateFormat("dd/MM/yyyy");
           try {
               return dateFormat.parse(source);
           } catch (ParseException e) {
               throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid date format");
           }
       }
   }
   ```

2. **Register Your Custom Converter**:
   Update your Spring configuration to include the new custom converter in your `conversionService`.

   ```xml
   <beans:bean id="conversionService" class="org.springframework.format.support.FormattingConversionServiceFactoryBean">
       <beans:property name="converters">
           <beans:list>
               <beans:bean class="com.example.NullableStringToDateConverter"/> <!-- Your custom converter -->
           </beans:list>
       </beans:property>
       <beans:property name="formatters">
           <beans:set>
               <beans:bean id="dateFormatter" class="org.springframework.format.datetime.DateFormatter">
                   <beans:constructor-arg>
                       <beans:value>dd/MM/yyyy</beans:value>
                   </beans:constructor-arg>
               </beans:bean>
           </beans:set>
       </beans:property>
   </beans:bean>
   ```

3. **Using the Converter**:
   With this setup, when the date is not provided (i.e., the request parameter is blank or not included), your custom converter will return null, which Spring MVC should handle correctly.

This approach ensures that any empty input for the date parameter is appropriately treated as null, thus solving your issue with allowing null dates in Spring MVC [SO-5982984][SO-55061837].

